d to form a pair with one of them. But they are single. So perhaps I need to adjust my hand.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different way to form melds. For example, if I don't form the [B5, B6, B7] Chow, but instead keep B5 and B6 for a different combination. Let's see. Suppose I don't take the Chi B5. Then my tiles are [W2, W3, W4, T7, T8, B2, B5, B6, B7, J3]. The existing meld is [W6, W7, W8]. So I need to form three more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- [W2, W3, W4] (Chow)\n- [B5, B6, B7] (Chow)\n- [B2] and [J3] as pairs? No, that's two single tiles. Wait, maybe I have a pair elsewhere. Let's check. The tiles are W2, W3, W4, T7, T8, B2, B5, B6, B7, J3. Are there any pairs? No, all are single. So I need to form a pair. But I don't have any pairs. Unless I can form a Pung or Kong. But I don't have three of any tile. So maybe this approach isn't working.\n\nWait, maybe I should consider forming a different meld. For example, if I have B2, maybe I can form a Pung if I have two more B2s. But I only have one B2. So that's not possible. Alternatively, maybe the T7 and T8 can form part of a Chow if I get a T9. But I don't have T9. Alternatively, maybe the J3 is part of a Pung, but I only have one J3.\n\nHmm. So if I take the Chi B5, I form the [B5, B6, B7] Chow, which would give me three Chows: [W6, W7, W8], [W2, W3, W4], and [B5, B6, B7]. Then the remaining tiles are B2 and J3. But I need a fourth meld and a pair. Wait, no, four melds and a pair. So after three Chows, I need one more meld and a pair. But I only have two tiles left. So perhaps I need to form a fourth meld and a pair. But with two tiles, that's impossible. Therefore, taking the Chi B5 might not be the right move here because it leaves me with an incomplete hand.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different way to arrange the melds. For example, if I don't form the [B5, B6, B7] Chow, but instead use some other combination. Let's see. Suppose I have the existing [W6, W7, W8] and [W2, W3, W4] as two Chow